course: dictionary
lesson: 12207
title: Congratulations and good news
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Congratulations is a word you say when something good has happened to someone. It shows you are happy for them. «Congratulations on winning the race!» The clue is winning the race: the person did something good, and you are cheering for them.
explain: Look at the words around it. «The teacher said, "Congratulations to the whole class for your hard work."» The words hard work and the class tell you that congratulations praises people for trying hard and doing well.
explain: Try swapping in your meaning. «Well done, I am so pleased for you» fits in place of congratulations in both sentences, so your meaning is right. This check helps whenever a word is long and new.
fact: Congratulations means words that praise someone for a success or for good news.
fact: You can say «Congratulations!» on its own, or with «on» or «to»: «congratulations on your new baby».
fact: Congratulations is a plural noun, so people usually use it in the plural form.
fact: Good times to say it include a win, a birthday, a new baby, or passing a test.
fact: Words with a similar meaning are «well done» and «good on you».
exercise: «Congratulations on winning the race!» What does congratulations mean here? | Goodbye, see you at school | Please run faster next time | *Well done, I am happy for you about your win | I am sorry you lost the race
exercise: The teacher says, «Congratulations to the whole class for your hard work.» Why does the teacher say it? | *Because the class worked hard and did well | Because the lesson is over | Because it is raining outside | Because the class is leaving school
exercise: Which is a good time to say congratulations? | *Your friend passes a spelling test | It is raining | You are late for school | Your friend loses a game
test: Your cousin has a new baby. What do you say? | Goodbye | Go away | *Congratulations! | Sorry
test: Which word means nearly the same as congratulations? | *Well done | Cold | Whisper | Goodbye
exam: Sam writes to his friend: «Congratulations on your good report!» What is Sam doing? | Saying sorry | Telling a joke | Asking for help | *Praising his friend for good news
